    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The protein universe, or the set of all proteins of all biological organisms on earth, is vast, rapidly growing, and largely uncharacterized.  To better study the millions of proteins that have been sequenced, we utilize domain architectures, which annotate proteins with sequence profiles that correlate with structural domains.  If proteins are considered to be the language of a genome, we can think of sequence profiles as “words” and domain architectures with more than one sequence profile as “sentences” (Scaiewicz & Levitt, 2015). This linguistic analogy allows us to use text classifiers to classify proteins into taxonomic groups or kingdoms. We built three text classifiers: (1) dependent only on word content of a domain architecture and based off Naïve Bayes, (2) dependent only on word order in a domain architecture and based off Markov chains, and (3) a combination of the first two. We found that Archaea and Viruses performed better with the order-independent classifier (1), indicating that their vocabulary is the defining feature of their language. Eukaryotes and Prokaryotes performed better using the combination method (3), showing that word order (a very simple grammar) also plays a role in their languages. We found significant relationships between fraction of unique words and sentences in a language to the ability to predict that language. The relationship was linear between unique sentences and prediction ability but logarithmic between unique words and prediction ability. Kingdoms of Life (Eukaryotes, Prokaryotes, Viruses and Archaea) occupied the horizontal portion of the logarithmic curve that approached perfect prediction, but plants also were on that part of the curve (between Archaea and Viruses), establishing the vocabulary of plants as almost as distinct as that of a kingdom. Language analysis on proteins has assisted in identifying trends in a vast, poorly understood, but very important area of science.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
